the american secretary of state landed  in doha for this key moment  maybe the beginning of the end of  decades of conflict in afghanistan  the qatari capital is the setting for  the talks a process of reconciliation  between afghanistan's warring factions  the taliban and representatives of the  afghan government  together we've come to this country to  achieve a dignified and lasting peace  with goodwill and good intention  to stop the 40 years of bloodshed and  achieve country-wide  lasting peace the mullahs whose number  had harbored al qaeda and osama bin  laden nearly 20 years ago  in the same room now as america's  secretary of state  and the afghan government who they have  fought for so long  the taliban's representative pledged  peace to the weary and skeptical  afghan people we want to give them this  assurance that with full honesty  we continue the afghan peace negotiation  and we will try for peace and  tranquility  we will pay for the ground in  afghanistan  there have been huge compromises to get  to this point including controversial  prisoner releases  until just a few days ago the taliban  were still demanding  that more of their fighters be released  we welcome the taliban commitment not to  host  international terrorist groups including  al qaeda  nor to allow them to use afghan  territory to train  recruit or to fund raise  we welcome the same commitments by the  government islamic republic  afghanistan that they should never  permit their nation to serve as a base  for international terrorists  to threaten other countries the  americans have already signed a separate  agreement with the taliban setting out a  timetable for u.s  forces to withdraw the many nations who  fought the taliban will now resist  or accept an uncomfortable feeling of  futility  for britain it was a war in which the  government infamously hoped that not a  shot would be fired  instead towns like sangin musa kala  kajaki became synonymous with hard  battles  and many lives lost 15 years on these  places are all now back in taliban  control  [Music]  now they are pledging peace but in a  country that's been a battleground for  centuries not decades  it'll be hard to find mark stone sky  news
 

